WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.561
A REST API route is a security boundary
because it sits between outside requests and

2
00:00:04.641 --> 00:00:06.207
trusted application behavior.

3
00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:08.897
A Go API may receive calls from browsers,
mobile apps,

4
00:00:08.977 --> 00:00:12.400
internal services, automation, partner
systems, or scheduled jobs.

5
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.787
Even when an API is called internal, its
handlers still accept data,

6
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:17.897
identity context, headers, parameters,

7
00:00:17.977 --> 00:00:21.632
and workflow instructions from outside the
handler's immediate control.

8
00:00:21.712 --> 00:00:24.438
Each route becomes part of the system's
security model.

9
00:00:24.518 --> 00:00:27.535
A handler parses input, chooses status
codes, loads records,

10
00:00:27.615 --> 00:00:31.907
enforces authorization, calls business
logic, writes state, and shapes the response.

11
00:00:31.987 --> 00:00:35.758
A small mistake in that path can expose
too much data, mutate the wrong object,

12
00:00:35.838 --> 00:00:37.810
leak a sensitive value through an error,

13
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.907
or let one tenant's request influence
another tenant's data.

14
00:00:40.987 --> 00:00:42.959
Go helps by keeping control flow direct.

15
00:00:43.039 --> 00:00:45.824
The standard HTTP library is clear, errors
are explicit,

16
00:00:45.904 --> 00:00:47.992
and handlers can be small enough to
review.

17
00:00:48.072 --> 00:00:50.914
But Go does not automatically decide which
caller may act,

18
00:00:50.994 --> 00:00:54.881
which fields are safe, which requests are
too large, which logs need redaction,

19
00:00:54.961 --> 00:00:56.934
or which defaults belong in production.

20
00:00:57.014 --> 00:00:59.334
A secure REST API makes safe behavior
explicit.

21
00:00:59.414 --> 00:01:01.560
Inputs are validated before sensitive use.

22
00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:05.121
Authorization happens on the server and
close to the protected action.

23
00:01:05.201 --> 00:01:07.057
Responses return only intended data.

24
00:01:07.137 --> 00:01:08.471
Failures are predictable.

25
00:01:08.551 --> 00:01:12.206
Logging supports investigation without
becoming a second exposure path.

26
00:01:12.286 --> 00:01:14.258
The practical goal is maintainability.

27
00:01:14.338 --> 00:01:17.703
A reviewer should understand what the
route trusts, what it rejects,

28
00:01:17.783 --> 00:01:19.581
what it changes, and what it returns.

29
00:01:19.661 --> 00:01:23.780
Security improves when handlers are
predictable under both normal use and failure.
